  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 24 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
